1. Lists in HTML

Lists are used to display a group of related items.

HTML provides three types of lists:

A. Ordered List <ol>

An ordered list displays items with numbers.

<h2>Programming Languages</h2>

<ol>
    <li>Java</li>
    <li>Python</li>
    <li>JavaScript</li>
    <li>C++</li>
</ol>

Output:

Java
Python
JavaScript
C++
<ol> → Ordered List
<li> → List Item
B. Unordered List <ul>

An unordered list displays items using bullets.

<h2>Frontend Technologies</h2>

<ul>
    <li>HTML</li>
    <li>CSS</li>
    <li>JavaScript</li>
    <li>React JS</li>
</ul>

Output:

HTML
CSS
JavaScript
React JS
<ul> → Unordered List
<li> → List Item
C. Description List <dl>

A description list is used for terms and their descriptions.

<dl>
    <dt>HTML</dt>
    <dd>Used to create the structure of a webpage.</dd>

    <dt>CSS</dt>
    <dd>Used to design and style a webpage.</dd>
</dl>
<dl> → Description List
<dt> → Description Term
<dd> → Description Details
2. Tables in HTML

Tables are used to display data in rows and columns.

Basic Table
<table border="1">

    <tr>
        <th>ID</th>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Course</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>101</td>
        <td>Rahul</td>
        <td>Java</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>102</td>
        <td>Priya</td>
        <td>Python</td>
    </tr>

</table>
Important Tags
Tag	Meaning
<table>	Creates table
<tr>	Table Row
<th>	Table Heading
<td>	Table Data
Table with Caption
<table border="1">

    <caption>Student Details</caption>

    <tr>
        <th>ID</th>
        <th>Name</th>
        <th>Marks</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>1</td>
        <td>Amit</td>
        <td>85</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<caption> gives a title to the table.

3. Forms in HTML

Forms are used to collect information from users.

For example:

Student registration
Login
Contact form
Feedback form
Job application
Basic Form
<form>

    <label>Name:</label>
    <input type="text">

    <br><br>

    <label>Email:</label>
    <input type="email">

    <br><br>

    <label>Password:</label>
    <input type="password">

    <br><br>

    <button type="submit">Register</button>

</form>
Important Form Tags
Tag	Purpose
<form>	Creates a form
<label>	Label for input
<input>	Takes user input
<textarea>	Multi-line input
<select>	Creates dropdown
<option>	Dropdown option
<button>	Creates button
Example: Student Registration Form
<form>

    <h2>Student Registration</h2>

    <label>Full Name:</label>
    <input type="text" name="name">

    <br><br>

    <label>Email:</label>
    <input type="email" name="email">

    <br><br>

    <label>Password:</label>
    <input type="password" name="password">

    <br><br>

    <label>Gender:</label>

    <input type="radio" name="gender" value="male">
    <label>Male</label>

    <input type="radio" name="gender" value="female">
    <label>Female</label>

    <br><br>

    <label>Course:</label>

    <select name="course">
        <option>Java</option>
        <option>Python</option>
        <option>React JS</option>
    </select>

    <br><br>

    <button type="submit">Register</button>

</form>
4. Input Types in HTML

The <input> element supports different type values.

Common Input Types
Input Type	Purpose
text	Single-line text
password	Password
email	Email address
number	Numbers
date	Date
time	Time
radio	Select one option
checkbox	Select multiple options
file	Upload file
color	Select color
range	Select range
tel	Telephone number
url	Website URL
search	Search field
submit	Submit form
reset	Reset form
button	Normal button
Example
<form>

    Name:
    <input type="text">

    <br><br>

    Email:
    <input type="email">

    <br><br>

    Password:
    <input type="password">

    <br><br>

    Age:
    <input type="number">

    <br><br>

    Birth Date:
    <input type="date">

    <br><br>

    Time:
    <input type="time">

    <br><br>

    Upload Resume:
    <input type="file">

    <br><br>

    Select Color:
    <input type="color">

    <br><br>

    <input type="submit" value="Submit">

    <input type="reset" value="Reset">

</form>
Radio Button

Radio buttons are generally used when the user should select one option.

<p>Gender:</p>

<input type="radio" name="gender" value="male">
<label>Male</label>

<input type="radio" name="gender" value="female">
<label>Female</label>

The same name="gender" groups the radio buttons together.

Checkbox

Checkboxes allow the user to select multiple options.

<p>Skills:</p>

<input type="checkbox" name="skill" value="java">
<label>Java</label>

<input type="checkbox" name="skill" value="html">
<label>HTML</label>

<input type="checkbox" name="skill" value="css">
<label>CSS</label>